From 77ccdcc004144ccba1dc88865be39ce0fdc08b0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:12:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 17/21] l2r-local: entry 23-i: worklist release: the last child
 continues by a tail call

An optimization of the release functions (23-d): in the worklist order,
a freed cell's last dead Worklist child is released next, so a chain is
a loop. The release pushed that child like the others and returned, and
the drain popped it right away: a runtime call to defer, a pop and an
indirect call per level of a chain.

`<dtor>.worklist.release(box)` now expands the record itself instead of
calling the walk (it is glue too). Per arm of a variant (or for a
compound record), it releases the members as the walk does, except the
arm's last member box that the walk would defer (a plain or nullable
box of a Worklist record), provided no later member may push work onto
the drain: after the other members, it loads that child, frees the box,
decrements the child and, if it is dead, calls the child's release as a
`musttail` call (the attribute TailDrop already uses). The drain would
have popped that child next, since nothing was pushed after it; moving
its decrement after the later members changes nothing observable, since
their drops have no effect (a member counts as possibly pushing, and
keeps the child deferred in field order, if it can reach a Worklist
record, a closure or an FFI object, whose hooks may push: mayPush, one
reachability walk per query like 23-h's holdsWorklistBox). The
walk, which the entry runs on a payload it does not own, is unchanged.

WebAssembly has tail calls only with the `tail-call` feature, which the
module does not record, so on wasm targets the release keeps calling the
walk and freeing the box. (TailDrop's `musttail` has the same limit:
upstream's tail drop of a list fails in llc for wasm32-wasip1 without
`+tail-call`; not changed here.)

Instructions per freed cell (plain Reussir microbenchmark, 10^6 levels,
cachegrind, before / after): right-deep tree 59.8 / 40.8, 64-arm enum
chain 62.4 / 40.8, right-nested compositions 80.3 / 43.3, balanced tree
59.8 / 50.3, rose tree 63.3 / 51.5; left-deep trees and left-nested
compositions, whose last member is a leaf, unchanged (23-j).

Tests: conversion/worklist_drop.mlir checks the release (deferral of the
first child, the box member released, the free, the musttail call), a
record whose `[value]` member of the cycle after the last child keeps
that child deferred (no tail call; a review found the tail call taken
there, which reordered the subtrees: runtime/drop_member_order.mlir's
OrderTri case, 23-g), and, in a wasm32 module, the release calling the
walk;
codegen/retcon_drop_targets.rr checks the musttail call on six targets
and the walk call on wasm32-wasip1.
